{
 "id": "1921-yokoyama-paired",
 "name": "Paired comparisons of colours and forms (Yokoyama 1921)",
 "description": "Faithful replication of Part I (Series S and N) of Matsusaburo Yokoyama's \"Affective tendency as conditioned by color and form\" (American Journal of Psychology 32, 1921, pp. 81–107; Clark University, 1919–20), a classic use of the method of paired comparisons. Two stimuli appear side by side in two square windows of a grey screen for three seconds after a ready signal; the participant chooses the more pleasant one (left / right, or \"can't decide\"). Series S compares seven 5 × 5 cm coloured squares (red, orange, yellow, green, cyan, blue, purple) and Series N seven black outline forms (triangle, circle, annular sector, pentagon, hexagon, upright rectangle, ellipse) drawn to the sizes given in the paper; every pair appears in both left–right orders (42 comparisons per series) in haphazard order with no stimulus in two successive comparisons. Each trial stores the pair, the choice and the reaction time, so preference orders (percent of comparisons won) can be computed as in the paper. Differences from the original study: (1) colours are screen approximations of the Milton-Bradley papers, matched to the brightness values reported in the paper; (2) responses by button or arrow key instead of a reaction key and spoken \"left/right\"; (3) Parts II–V (colour–form combinations, repetition after five months) and introspective reports are left out; untrained participants instead of five trained observers; gender, age and colour vision are asked at the end. Public domain source (JSTOR Early Journal Content).",
